# GraphQL Conventions (gqlgen)

## File Layout

```
cmd/graphql/
  server.go              — HTTP server setup, middleware chain
  graph/
    schema.graphqls      — Schema definition (single file)
    resolver.go          — Resolver struct with dependencies (hand-edited)
    schema.resolvers.go  — Resolver implementations (gqlgen regenerates scaffold)
    generated.go         — DO NOT EDIT (gqlgen output)
    helpers.go           — Shared helpers (hashing, token generation)
    model/models_gen.go  — DO NOT EDIT (gqlgen output)
  dataloader/
    dataloaders.go       — Loader registry
    middleware.go        — Dataloader injection middleware
    *loader.go           — Individual loader implementations
```

## Resolver Pattern

- The `Resolver` struct holds all dependencies (DB pool, Redis, Store, Logger, keys)
- Resolvers extract auth claims from context — never trust client input for identity
- Use `auth.UserIDKey`, `auth.EntityIDKey`, `auth.RoleKey` context keys

```go
userID, ok := ctx.Value(auth.UserIDKey).(uuid.UUID)
if !ok {
    return nil, fmt.Errorf("unauthorized")
}
```

## N+1 Prevention

Use dataloaders for any field that resolves a related entity. Loader pattern:

```go
loader := dataloader.For(ctx).UserLoader
thunk := loader.Load(ctx, dataloader.StringKey(id))
result, err := thunk()
```

## Middleware Chain

`Auth → RBAC → Dataloader → GraphQL Handler`

Auth middleware is non-blocking — it injects claims if valid, skips if missing. Protected resolvers check for claims presence. Public resolvers (login, signup, introspection) work without auth.

## Error Handling

- Return `fmt.Errorf("internal server error")` for user-facing errors
- Log actual errors with `r.Logger.Error(...)` before returning
- Never leak internal details (DB errors, stack traces) to the client
